      "explanationAfterStep": "TFA protonates the carbonyl oxygen. The positive charge is shared over oxygen, the carbonyl carbon and nitrogen."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "tert-butyl-cation-leaves",
      "fromStateId": "protonated-carbamate",
      "toStateId": "tert-butyl-cation",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "σ C10-O14 -> π O14-C15",
        "π C15-O16 -> lp O16"
      ],
      "explanationAfterStep": "The tert-butyl group leaves as a tertiary carbocation, and a carbamic acid forms. This is an SN1-type ionisation: it works because the cation is tertiary. A methyl or ethyl carbamate does not come off this way."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "isobutene-forms",
      "fromStateId": "tert-butyl-cation",
      "toStateId": "isobutene",
      "stepKind": "polar",
      "routePreference": "preferred",
      "canonicalArrows": [
        "lp O41 -> σ O41-H9",
        "σ C11-H9 -> π C10-C11"
      ],
      "explanationAfterStep": "Trifluoroacetate removes a proton from the cation (E1): isobutene, a gas. Some cation is also trapped by trifluoroacetate or by nucleophiles in the molecule, which is why cation scavengers are added in peptide synthesis."
    },
    {
      "stepKey": "nitrogen-protonated",
