  "mechanismSummary": "TFA protonates the carbamate C=O. The tert-butyl group leaves as a stable tertiary cation, which loses a proton to give isobutene. The carbamic acid takes a proton on nitrogen and loses CO₂, releasing the amine, which ends up as its trifluoroacetate salt. Boc comes off in acid because the tert-butyl cation is stable; it survives base, nucleophiles and hydrogenation. Fmoc is the opposite: stable to acid, removed by base. Using both on one molecule is orthogonal protection.",
